  abstract = {Nodes and edges are the elemental building blocks of networks, and their accurate definition is of paramount importance for any valid graphical theoretical model of network organization. A particular challenge for neuroscience is the multiscale organization of the brain, which means that networks can be validly defined at the microscale level of cells and synapses, the mesoscale level of cell populations and axons, and the macroscale level of brain regions and fiber tracts. In this chapter, we provide an overview of different methods for mapping brain connectivity, and defining nodes and edges at each scale, while highlighting the strengths and advantages of each approach. We explain differences between the three major classes of brain connectivity – structural, functional, and effective – and consider the limitations associated with their measurement at each resolution scale.},
