  abstract = {A natural question to ask in a network analysis is whether some nodes are more important for network function than others. This question is intimately related to the concept of topological centrality: the capacity of a node to influence, or be influenced by, other nodes by virtue of its connectivity. In this chapter, we consider three broad classes of centrality measures: those based on degree, including eigenvector and PageRank centrality; those based on closeness, which are related to the average shortest path length of a node; and those based on betweenness. We show how these distinct classes of measures assume different models of information flow on the network, and consider which measures are the most appropriate for brain networks. We also examine how these measures can be used to define network hubs.},
  isbn = {978-0-12-407908-3},
