  urldate = {2025-06-04},
  abstract = {In many networks, nodes can aggregate into subsets that play an influential role in network function. This chapter considers different methods for defining these subsets. We start with the coarsest level, in which the analysis of connected components can be used to determine whether or not a network is fragmented, and to understand its robustness to random failure or targeted attack through the lens of percolation theory. We consider methods for decomposing a network into a strongly connected core of topologically central nodes and a sparsely connected periphery. These approaches include those based on block model optimization, core decomposition, and the identification of so-called knotty centres using a measure of betweenness centrality. We then turn to the analysis of rich-club organization, which examines whether the highest degree nodes of the network are more strongly or densely connected with each other than expected by chance. A considerable body of evidence indicates that rich-club organization is a robust feature of brain networks, and plays a vital role in the integration of segregated network elements.},
